<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout>
    <title>Section 1.4: Linear Functions</title>
    <p>
    <alert>
        MTH 124
    </alert>
    </p>
    <p workspace="0.15in">
        Functions that are straight lines are both the simplest and the most
        important functions in mathematics!
        And linear functions are directly related to the average rate of
        change...
    </p>

    <definition workspace="0.15in">
        <title>Definition 1.4.3</title>

        <statement>
            <p>
                A <term>linear</term> function has a constant average rate of
                change.
                So for <em>any</em> initial and final inputs <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> (as
                long as <m>a\ne b</m>), it follows that
                <md>
                    \frac{f(b) - f(a)}{b-a} = m
                </md>
                for a fixed constant <m>m</m>.
                This <m>m</m> is called the <term>slope</term> of the linear
                function <m>f</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
        There are many formats to find the equation of a line.
        The following is very useful because you only need to know one point and
        the slope!
    </p>

    <assemblage>
        <title>Point-slope form of a line</title>

        <p>
            A line with slope <m>m</m> (equivalently, average rate of change
            <m>m</m> ) that passes through the point <m>(x_0,y_0)</m> has
            equation
            <md>
                y = y_0 + m(x-x_0).
            </md>
        </p>
    </assemblage>

    <activity>
        <title>Activity 1.4.2</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                Find an equation for the line that is determined by the
                following conditions; write your answer in point-slope form
                wherever possible.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The line with slope <m>\frac37</m> that passes through
                    <m>(-11,-17)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-4-soln">
                <p>
                    <m>m=\frac37, \ (x_0,y_0)=(-11,-17)</m>.  So the line is 
                    <md>
                        y=-17+\frac37(x+11)
                    </md>
                    
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The line passing through the points <m>(-2,5)</m> and
                    <m>(3,-1)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-4-soln">
                <p>
                    <m>m=\frac{5-(-1)}{-2-3}=\frac{6}{-5}=-\frac65, \ (x_0,y_0)=(-2,5)</m>.  
                    So the line is 
                    <md>
                        y=5-\frac65(x-(-2))
                    </md>
                    or if you use <m>(x_0,y_0)=(3,-1)</m> then the line is the same as 
                    <md>
                        y=-1-\frac65(x-3).
                    </md>
                    These are actually the same.  You only need one.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The line passing through <m>(4,9)</m> that is parallel to
                    the line <m>2x - 3y = 5</m>.
                    (What do you know about slopes of parallel lines?)
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-4-soln">
                <p>
                    Parallel lines have the same slope, so rewriting the line 
                    so it is in a form where we can pick out the slope:
                    <md>
                        2x - 3y = 5 \implies 3y=2x-5 \implies y=\frac23 x - \frac53
                    </md>
                    so the slope is <m>m=\frac23</m>.  The point is <m>(x_0,y_0)=(4,9)</m> so
                    <md>
                        y=9+\frac23(x-4)
                    </md>
                    
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.5in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Explain why the function <m>f</m> given by the table below
                    appears to be linear and find a formula for <m>f(x)</m>.
                </p>


                    <tabular margins="0% 70%">
                        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                            <cell><m>x</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>f(x)</m></cell>
                        </row>

                        <row>
                            <cell>1</cell>
                            <cell>7</cell>
                        </row>

                        <row>
                            <cell>3</cell>
                            <cell>3</cell>
                        </row>

                        <row>
                            <cell>4</cell>
                            <cell>1</cell>
                        </row>

                        <row>
                            <cell>7</cell>
                            <cell>-5</cell>
                        </row>
                    </tabular>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-4-soln">
                <p>
                    As <m>x</m> goes up by 1, <m>f(x)</m> goes down by 2, so the function
                    has a constant slope of <m>m=-2</m> and thus is linear. Choose any point in 
                    the table and use point-slope form.  I'll choose <m>(1,7)</m>:
                    <md>
                        y=7-2(x-1).
                    </md>
                    
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find a formula for the linear function shown in the graph
                    below.
                </p>

                <image margins="0% 70%" width="30%">
                    <shortdescription>A linear function h(x).</shortdescription>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-1-4-1">
                        <diagram dimensions="(250,250)" margins="5">
                            <definition>
                                h(x)=-1/3*(x-1)+2
                            </definition>
                            <coordinates bbox="[-5,-3,5,5]">
                                <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" hticks="(-4,2,4)" vticks="(-2,2,4)" decorations="yes"/>
                                <graph function="h"/>
                                <label anchor="(3,2.5)">
                                    <m>
                                        y=h(x)
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
                </image>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-4-soln">
                <p>
                    Choose two "easy-to-see" points to find the slope. Ex: <m>(1,2)</m> and 
                    <m>(4,1)</m>.  Then <m>m=\frac{2-1}{1-4}=-\frac13</m>. (Or you can count from the graph.)
                    Choose any point, I'll use <m>(1,2)</m>.  Then the equation is
                    <md>
                        y=2-\frac13(x-1).
                    </md>
                    
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </activity>

    <assemblage>
        <title>Slope-intercept form</title>

        <p>
            For the line with slope <m>m</m> that passes through the point
            <m>(0,y_0)</m>, its equation is
            <md>
                y = mx+y_0.
            </md>
        </p>
    </assemblage>

    <p>
        We usually see this as <m>y = mx+b</m>, where <m>b</m> is the <m>y-</m>
        intercept, and <m>m</m> is the same slope.
    </p>

    <activity>
        <title>Activity 1.4.3. Linear Functions in Context</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                The summit of Africa’s largest peak, Mt.
                Kilimanjaro, has two main ice fields and a glacier at its peak.
                Geologists measured the ice cover in the year 2000 ( <m>t=0</m>
                ) to be approximately <m>1951</m> <m>m^2</m>; in the year 2007,
                the ice cover measured <m>1555</m> <m> m^2</m>.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose that the amount of ice cover at the peak of Mt.
                    Kilimanjaro is changing at a constant average rate from year
                    to year.
                    Find a linear model <m>A=f(t)</m> whose output is the area
                    of the ice cover, <m>A</m>, in square meters in year
                    <m>t</m> (where <m>t</m> is the number of years after 2000).
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-4-soln">
                <p>
                    There are two points we can pick out from the context: <m>(0,1951)</m>
                    and <m>(7,1555)</m>. Then <m>{m=\frac{1555-1951}{7-0}=-\frac{396}{7}}</m>.
                    If we choose point <m>(0,1951)</m>, then point-slope form and y-intercept
                    form are the same!  (Though you can choose the other point if you like.)
                    <md>A=f(t)=1951 - \frac{396}{7}(t-0).</md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What do the slope and <m>A</m>-intercept mean in the model
                    you found in (a)?
                    In particular, what are the units on the slope?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-4-soln">
                <p>
                    Slope is the average rate of change, units m^2 / year, so it's the average loss
                    of ice cover in m^2 / year.
                </p>
                <p>
                    The A-intercept is the initial amount of ice cover in year <m>t=0</m> (year 2000).
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Compute <m>f(17)</m>.
                    What does this quantity measure?
                    Write a complete sentence to explain.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-4-soln">
                <p>
                    Plug in: <m>{f(17) = 1951 - \frac{396}{7}(17)\approx 989 m^2}</m>. In 2017 the model 
                    predicts that the ice field at the top of Mt. Kilimanjaro will have an area of 989 square meters.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If the model holds further into the future, when do we
                    predict the ice cover will vanish?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-4-soln">
                <p>
                    We look for the time when <m>f(t)=0</m>, that is <m>{0 = 1951 - \frac{396}{7}t}</m>. 
                    Solving, we find <m>t \approx 34.5 years</m>. So halfway through the year 2034.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    In light of your work above, what is a reasonable domain to
                    use for the model <m>A=f(t)</m>?
                    What is the corresponding range?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-4-soln">
                <p>
                    Domain: <m>0\le t \le 34.5</m> (or <m>[0,34.5]</m>) since negative years or years after the ice cover vanishes don't make sense.

                </p>
                <p>
                    Range: <m>0\le A \le 1951</m> (or <m>[0,1951]</m>) since once the ice cover completely 
                    melts you can output 0 square meters of ice, and the max is year 0 which is 1951 square 
                    meters, since the ice only melts it will not get higher.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </activity>

    <activity>
        <title>Activity 1.4.4. Context</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                In each of the following prompts, we investigate linear
                functions in context.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A town’s population initially has 28750 people present and
                    then grows at a constant rate of <m>825</m> people per year.
                    Find a linear model <m>P=f(t)</m> for the number of people
                    in the town in year <m>t</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-4-soln">
                <p>
                    Slope: <m>825</m>, P-intercept: <m>28750</m>. So slope-intercept form gives
                    <md>
                        P=f(t)=825t+28,750.
                    </md>
                    
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A different town’s population <m>Q</m> is given by the
                    function <m>Q=g(t) = 42505-465t</m>.
                    What is the slope of this function and what is its meaning
                    in the model?
                    Write a complete sentence to explain.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-4-soln">
                <p>
                    Slope is -465, with units of people per year. It means the town population
                    decreases by about 465 people each year.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A spherical tank is being drained with a pump.
                    Initially the tank is full with <m>\frac{32\pi}{3}</m> cubic
                    feet of water.
                    Assume the tank is drained at a constant rate of <m>1.2</m>
                    cubic feet per minute.
                    Find a linear model <m>V=p(t)</m> for the total amount of
                    water in the tank at time <m>t</m>.
                    In addition, what is a reasonable approximate domain for the
                    model?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-4-soln">
                <p>
                    Slope is -1.2, and the V-intercept is <m>\frac{32\pi}{3}</m>. So slope-intercept 
                    form gives <md>
                        V=p(t)=-1.2t+\frac{32\pi}{3}.
                    </md>
                    The model is no longer valid once the tank is empty, which takes <m>\frac{32\pi/3}{1.2}\approx
                    27.9</m> minutes. So a reasonable domain for times that make sense: <m>{0\le t \le 27.9}</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A conical tank is being filled in such a way that the height
                    of the water in the tank, <m>h</m> (in feet), at time
                    <m>t</m> (in minutes) is given by the function
                    <m>h=q(t)=0.65t</m>.
                    What can you say about how the water level is rising?
                    Write at least one careful sentence to explain.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-4-soln">
                <p>
                    The tank is initially empty since the initial height of the water is 0
                    (<m>q(0)=0</m>), and the height of the water in the tank is increasing
                    at a constant rate of 0.65 ft per minute. 
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose we know that a <m>5</m>-year old car’s value is
                    <m> 10200 </m>,
 and that after <m> 10
                    </m>
 years its value is <m> 4600</m>.
                    Assuming that the car’s value depreciates linearly, find a
                    function <m>C=L(t)</m> whose output is the value of the car
                    in year <m>t</m>.
                    What is a reasonable domain for the model?
                    What is the value and meaning of the slope of the line?
                    Write at least one careful sentence to explain.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-4-soln">
                <p>
                    We have two points from the model: <m>{(5,10200)}</m> and 
                    <m>{(10,4600)}</m>, so the slope is 
                    <m>{m=\frac{4600-10200}{10-5}=-1120}</m>.  Picking the first point
                    for no reason whatsoever (and including a simplified version because why not):
                    <md>
                        C=L(t)=10200-1120(t-5) = 15800-1120t.
                    </md>
                    The reasonable domain ends when cost (<m>C</m>) is 0, that is when 
                    <m>{0=15800-1120t}</m>, which happens when <m>t=14.1</m> years, so the 
                    domain would be <m>0\le t \le 14.1</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </activity>
</handout>